  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM EST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nEarly this afternoon surface and satellite analysis showed a large \nstorm system moving up the Eastern Seaboard with extensive \ncloudiness pushing westwards into the U.P. Additionally lake effect \nclouds were pushing west (under easterly flow) off of the Great \nLakes but no precipitation was occuring as the unstable layer \nremained shallow. Temperatures had warmed into the low 30s in the \nwest and as warm as the upper 30s near the Bay of Green Bay and \nNorthern Lake Michigan.\n\nThrough early next week a typical El Nino jet pattern will be set up \nwith a splitting jet along the west coast keeping the subtropical \njet over the southern tier of the U.S. and the polar jet well north \nof the Great Lakes. Brief intrusions of colder air can be expected \nas shortwaves move along the polar jet but overall the cold air will \nremain bottled up over northern Canada and the Arctic. The result \nwill be daytime highs in the upper 30s and 40s, 5-10 degrees above \naverage. Lows will mainly be in the 20s to low 30s (warmest east and \nlakeside) which is 10-15 degrees above average. \n\nLow pressure moving up the eastern seaboard will spread cloudiness \nwestward through the night helping to limit cooling with lows \ndipping into the low 20s interior west and near 30 in the east. \nPrecipitation with this system is expected to stay south and east of \nthe U.P. with dry weather overnight into Friday. A break in the \nclouds can be expected during the day on Friday but clouds will \nagain increase as a clipper low moves into Manitoba. This will also \nbring southerly flow into the area on Friday night into Saturday \nwith a continued moderation in temperatures to well above average, \nhighs in the 40s for Saturday. As a warm front pushes across the \narea Friday night a band of light precipitation is expected to \naccompany it, mainly in the western and central U.P. Precipitation \nwill be in the liquid form but with surface temperatures below \nfreezing over the interior a very light glaze of ice possible. REFS \nprob for >0.01\" is only around 20%, thus not expecting widespread \nimpacts from any freezing rain that occurs. \n\nA cold front will sweep across the U.P. Sunday morning as the \nclipper moves east to the Hudson Bay. A secondary low will develop \nover the Southern Plains Saturday night and track east-northeast \nalong the Ohio River Valley Sunday. Most of the synoptic scale \nforcing and moisture will remain south and east of the area though \nthe eastern U.P. could see a few light showers. As 850mb temps fall \nbehind the cold front lake effect rain showers will develop and \ntransition to snow showers through the day with continued caa, \nmainly for the west to west-northwest wind snow belts. Limited depth \nof moisture and dry low levels (classic inverted-v type soundings) \nshould limit the intensity and coverage of the lake effect showers \nand any snow accumulations should be minor (less than an inch). \n\nFor early through midweek next week the forecast confidence is \nsomewhat lower with deterministic and ensemble guidance spread on \nthe track and amplitude of the parade of shortwaves along the polar \njet. Ensemble means and blended guidance keeps highs well in the 30s \nto low 40s along with chance a 20-30% chance for rain and snow \nshowers Tuesday and Wednesday. At this time it doesn't appear that \nhigh impactful winter weather is on the horizon.",
